  planner_response {
    thinking: "**Defining Application Architecture**\n\nI\'m currently fleshing out the application\'s foundational structure. The `server.ts` file will house an Express server, employing Vite middleware during development and static files in production. Crucially, I\'m integrating a server-side Gemini API client, specifically `@google/genai`, which will utilize `gemini-3.7-flash` alongside Google Search grounding capabilities.\n\n\n**Planning API Endpoints**\n\nNow, I\'m defining specific API routes. I\'ve designed `/api/research/ask` for custom queries about swarm UIs. `/api/research/evaluate` will handle architecture evaluations. Finally, `/api/research/generate-config` will produce boilerplate configurations. I\'m also ensuring a fallback mechanism for when the Gemini API key is missing.\n\n\n\n**Comparing Multi-Agent Frameworks**\n\nI\'m now diving deeper into comparing existing multi-agent frameworks. I\'m focusing on their architectures, specifically looking at how they handle context sharing, dynamic agency routing, and handoff tools. I\'ve also examined their visual GUI capabilities, focusing on Autogen Studio, AgentSwarms Canvas, and Langflow, evaluating their strengths and weaknesses in multi-agent workflows. I am looking at how LangGraph enables them to perform real-time tracing.\n\n\n**Evaluating Multi-Agent Capabilities**\n\nI\'m now deep-diving into the strengths and limitations of several multi-agent frameworks, specifically analyzing Dify.ai\'s comprehensive LLM application and orchestration capabilities with its visual canvas and knowledge base RAG integration. I\'m assessing CrewAI\'s role-playing agent crews, and OpenHands\' (formerly OpenDevin) potential for autonomous coding swarms.\n\n\n**Analyzing Agent Tooling**\n\nI\'m now comparing various frameworks, examining tools like the workspace, Docker sandbox execution, a step-by-step reasoning tree, file diff viewers, and terminal inspectors. The event-stream runtime architecture is a key focus. I am also investigating PicoAgents Web UI, its multi-agent debugging capabilities, live chat streaming, and visual inspectors, which offer interactive debugging. I\'m also now analyzing the VoltAgent Platform and its supervisor orchestration and the reactive event engine.\n\n\n**Examining Framework Features**\n\nI\'m now investigating specific features of several frameworks. I\'m looking at conversational UIs with visualizers, human prompts, and multimodal support within the Apache 2.0 licensed systems like Call-tree streaming. I\'m also examining software development simulations with interactive visual office games, and the waterfall phase-driven multi-agent role-play. Finally, I\'m analyzing fast deployment of multi-agent workflows into web interfaces and REST APIs.\n\n\n**Developing Comparison Resources**\n\nI\'m now focusing on creating comparison resources. I\'ll provide tag chips, detailed modal breakdowns, installation commands, architecture diagrams, and track GitHub star trends. A side-by-side matrix will directly compare two to four projects, including scorecards and capability checklists such as multi-turn memory and local models. I\'m also planning an interactive swarm topology simulator, featuring a visual canvas with interactive agent nodes and switchable topologies. This simulator will show hierarchical supervisor, peer handoff swarm, sequential assembly line, and consensus/debate arena models with step-by-step simulation and live animation.\n\n\n"
    thinking_duration {
      seconds: 11
      nanos: 540069147
    }
    stop_reason: STOP_REASON_STOP_PATTERN
  }
}
